      kind: LessonKind.reading,
      xpReward: 10,
      readingPassage: ReadingPassageModel(
        titleEn: "Tin's Playground Survey",
        titleMy: "တင်၏ ကစားကွင်းစစ်တမ်း",
        passageEn:
            "During the Monday morning break, Tin decided to find out his classmates' favourite playground game. He asked fifteen classmates the very same question, one at a time -- \"Is your favourite playground game chinlone, skipping, or tag?\" -- and wrote down each answer exactly as he heard it, in order: chinlone, skipping, tag, chinlone, chinlone, skipping, tag, chinlone, skipping, skipping, chinlone, tag, skipping, chinlone, tag. By the time he reached the last classmate, his raw list held fifteen answers in total, but it was too jumbled to read at a glance -- he could not yet tell which game was the most popular just by looking at the order the answers came in.\n\nTo make sense of his list, Tin turned it into a tally chart. He drew three rows, one for chinlone, one for skipping, and one for tag, and then worked through his raw list one answer at a time, adding a single stroke to the matching row for every answer. The first four strokes in any row were drawn straight up and down, side by side; the fifth stroke was drawn diagonally across the first four, closing them into a neat \"gate of five\" that made the whole chart fast to count later. When Tin finished going through all fifteen answers, the chinlone row held one full gate of five plus one extra stroke, which is 5 + 1 = 6. The skipping row held exactly one full gate of five, which is 5. The tag row held four single strokes, not yet enough to close a gate, which is simply 4.\n\n\"Six plus five plus four,\" Tin said, adding the three totals together, \"is fifteen -- exactly the number of classmates I asked, so I haven't lost or double-counted a single answer.\" Moe Moe, looking over his shoulder, pointed out that his tally chart was already telling the same story a bar chart would tell, just without the drawn bars: if Tin used his tally totals as the heights of three bars, the chinlone bar would be the tallest at 6, the skipping bar would come next at 5, and the tag bar would be the shortest at 4. \"A tally chart and a bar chart use different pictures,\" she said, \"but they can show exactly the same information.\"",
        passageMy:
