            "Nova School's librarian was placing a big order for new books and asked Zin Min, who loved numbers, to help check the costs before the order was confirmed. The first item was a reference book set priced at 3,124 kyats each, and the librarian needed exactly 4 copies. Zin Min used short multiplication, carefully multiplying each digit of 3,124 by 4 and carrying the extra value into the next column, and found the total cost was 12,496 kyats. He double-checked by adding 3,124 four times, and the answer matched perfectly.\n\nNext came a much bigger order: 34 new reading-corner chairs, each costing 27 thousand kyats. Since both numbers had two digits, Zin Min knew this called for long multiplication rather than short multiplication. Using the column method, he multiplied 34 by 7 to get 238, then multiplied 34 by 20 to get 680, and added the two partial products together to make 918. Because each chair cost 27 thousand kyats, the total order came to 918 thousand kyats, or 918,000 kyats altogether.\n\nTo be absolutely sure, Zin Min checked his answer using the grid method as well. He split 34 into 30 and 4, and 27 into 20 and 7, then multiplied each pair: 30 × 20 = 600, 30 × 7 = 210, 4 × 20 = 80, and 4 × 7 = 28. Adding these four partial products together — 600 + 210 + 80 + 28 — gave exactly 918 again, matching his column method answer perfectly. Zin Min told the librarian that the grid method was slower but helped him see why the answer was correct, while the column method was faster once he trusted the process, and that knowing both gave him a way to always check his own work.",
