        passageEn:
            "Every summer, Zin Min travelled with her parents to her grandfather's mango orchard in Sagaing, where rows of trees hung heavy with ripe fruit just before the market truck arrived each morning. This year, her grandfather handed her a woven basket holding 138 freshly picked mangoes and asked her to pack them into boxes of 6 for the truck, since each box had to be exactly the same size to fit snugly on the truck's shelves. Zin Min set up her short division, writing 138 inside the bus-stop bracket and 6 outside it: 1 hundred divided by 6 gave 0 boxes with 1 hundred carried into the tens column, 13 tens divided by 6 gave 2 boxes with 1 ten remaining, and finally 18 ones divided by 6 gave exactly 3 with nothing left over. Altogether, she filled 23 full boxes with no mangoes left over.\n\nPleased with her quotient, Zin Min moved on to a second, smaller basket holding 97 mangoes, which her grandfather wanted packed the same way, 6 to a box. This time her short division gave a quotient of 16 boxes with a remainder of 1, since 6 × 16 = 96 and one lonely mango was left over. Zin Min knew the remainder could not simply vanish, so she asked her grandfather what to do with it. He laughed and said the leftover mango was hers to eat right there in the orchard, since a remainder in real life often becomes something useful rather than a number to ignore.\n\nLater that morning, Zin Min's grandfather explained that the market truck could carry a maximum of 25 boxes per trip, and the orchard had 140 boxes ready to go. Zin Min divided 140 by 25 and found a quotient of 5 with a remainder of 15, meaning 5 full trips of 25 boxes would carry 125 boxes, leaving 15 boxes still waiting. Since those 15 boxes could not be left behind, she realised the truck needed a sixth trip just to carry the remainder, even though that trip would not be completely full. 'That's the real skill of division,' her grandfather told her, 'not just finding the answer, but understanding what the remainder actually means for the boxes, the mangoes, or the trips still waiting.'",
