        passageEn:
            "Zin Min, a Year 5 student at Nova School, is helping his mother cook fish curry for his cousin's birthday party this weekend. The recipe card says one batch needs 2/5 kg of fish, and so many guests are coming that his mother wants to cook 4 batches. Zin Min uses his new skill of multiplying a fraction by a whole number: 2/5 x 4 = 8/5 kg, which he rewrites as the mixed number 1 3/5 kg of fish.\n\nWhile the fish is marinating, Zin Min's mother asks him to work out the garlic and onion. The recipe calls for 1/3 cup of chopped garlic, and only half as much onion. Zin Min multiplies two fractions together: 1/2 x 1/3 = 1/6 cup of onion. He is proud that he can now multiply a fraction by another fraction, not just by a whole number.\n\nFinally, Zin Min's grandmother brings out a tray of 24 fried fish balls she has already prepared for the party. She tells Zin Min that guests usually eat 3/4 of the fish balls during the party itself, and the rest are saved for breakfast the next morning. Zin Min finds 3/4 of 24 by multiplying: 3/4 x 24 = 18 fish balls eaten at the party, leaving 6 fish balls saved for breakfast. By the end of the afternoon, Zin Min has used all three fraction-multiplication skills to help plan the entire family feast.",
