            "On Saturday morning, Zin Min goes with her mother to the weekend market near Nova School to buy ingredients for a family breakfast. Her mother asks her to check how much rice they need: the recipe calls for 750 grams of rice, but the market only sells rice in whole kilogram bags. Zin Min remembers that 1000 g makes 1 kg, so she quickly works out that a 1 kg bag will be more than enough, with 250 g left over for another day.\n\nAt the ribbon stall, Zin Min wants to buy ribbon for a craft project at school. The stall owner measures ribbon in metres, but Zin Min's teacher asked for 350 centimetres. Since 100 cm makes 1 m, Zin Min converts and realises she needs 3.5 m of ribbon. She buys exactly that much, careful not to waste any of her pocket money.\n\nOn the way home, Zin Min notices an imported juice bottle with two labels: one says \"1 litre\" and the other says \"about 1.06 quarts,\" an imperial unit. Her mother explains that some countries still use imperial units like quarts, pounds, and miles, but Myanmar and most of the world use the metric system instead. Zin Min feels proud that her Week 21 conversion skills helped her shop smartly and understand the labels around her.",
        passageMy:
