            "Zin Min's family runs a small tea shop in their neighbourhood, and this month they were replacing the cracked floor tiles. The tile shop owner showed Zin Min a catalogue full of geometric patterns, and Zin Min recognised many of the shapes from his Nova School geometry lessons. One tile was a perfect triangle with all three sides the same length -- an equilateral triangle -- while another nearby tile had only two matching sides, making it an isosceles triangle.\n\nZin Min's father asked how many degrees were missing from one triangular tile, since its corner had chipped off before the angle number was printed. The tile clearly showed two angles: 72 degrees and 58 degrees. Remembering that the angles inside any triangle always add up to 180 degrees, Zin Min quickly subtracted: 180 minus 72 minus 58 equals 50 degrees. His father was impressed that geometry class was already useful at home.\n\nFor the border of the floor, the shop chose a row of quadrilateral tiles. Some were parallelograms, with two pairs of parallel, equal sides, while others were trapeziums, with only one pair of parallel sides. Zin Min carefully sorted the delivery boxes by shape so the workers could lay the correct pattern, proud that his knowledge of triangle and quadrilateral properties made him a genuinely useful helper for the family business.",
