            "One morning, Zin Min arrived at school to find two classmates, Thiri and Kaung, arguing loudly in front of the whiteboard. Someone had written the expression 8 + 2 x 5 on the board as a warm-up puzzle, and Thiri insisted the answer was 50, while Kaung was certain it was 18. Neither of them could convince the other, so they turned to Zin Min to settle the disagreement.\n\nZin Min explained that mathematics has a strict order for solving mixed expressions, remembered by the word BODMAS: Brackets first, then Orders (powers and roots), then Division and Multiplication, and finally Addition and Subtraction. Since 8 + 2 x 5 has no brackets, the multiplication had to be done before the addition. That meant 2 x 5 = 10 first, and then 8 + 10 = 18 -- Kaung was correct. Thiri had simply worked from left to right, calculating 8 + 2 = 10 and then 10 x 5 = 50, which broke the BODMAS rule.\n\nTo make sure everyone understood, Zin Min wrote a second puzzle on the board: how could brackets be added to 8 + 2 x 5 to make the answer actually equal 50? He showed the class that placing brackets around the first two numbers, (8 + 2) x 5, forced the addition to happen first, giving 10 x 5 = 50 -- exactly the answer Thiri had originally guessed, but only correct once brackets were properly used. Later that day, Zin Min used the same idea at the school canteen: a snack cost 300 kyat and a drink cost 500 kyat, and he bought 3 snacks and 1 drink. Without brackets, the total was 3 x 300 + 500 = 900 + 500 = 1,400 kyat, since multiplication always comes before addition -- even when there's a hungry customer waiting to pay.",
