        passageEn:
            "Zin Min's grandmother loved posing riddles before letting anyone eat her mangoes, and this week's puzzle was a proper algebra problem in disguise. \"I have a certain number of mangoes,\" she said, tapping the basket. \"If I add five more mangoes to what I already have, I will have thirteen mangoes in total. How many mangoes do I have right now?\"\n\nZin Min realised this could be written as an algebraic expression: if m stands for the unknown number of mangoes, then his grandmother's clue meant m + 5 = 13. Since he knew how to substitute values, he tried m = 8, and sure enough, 8 + 5 = 13 -- exactly matching her clue. His grandmother beamed and handed him the eighth mango as a prize.\n\nInspired by the riddle, Zin Min decided to use the same letter-for-unknown idea to help plan his class's snack stall at the upcoming school fair. The class had already saved 2,000 kyat from a previous event, and they planned to sell homemade rice crackers at 300 kyat each. Zin Min wrote a formula for the total money the stall would have: Total = 300n + 2,000, where n stood for the number of rice crackers sold.\n\nTo check the formula, Zin Min substituted n = 40, imagining a good sales day: Total = 300 x 40 + 2,000 = 12,000 + 2,000 = 14,000 kyat. His teacher was impressed that Zin Min had turned both a family riddle and a school fundraising plan into the very same kind of algebraic thinking -- letting a single letter stand for whatever number was still unknown.",
