            "Zin Min's father wanted to build a new vegetable bed in the shape of a rectangle with a triangular corner added on, fitting neatly into an oddly shaped corner of the garden. The rectangular part measured 10 m long and 4 m wide, giving an area of 10 x 4 = 40 m². The triangular corner had a base of 10 m (matching the rectangle's long side) and a height of 3 m, giving an area of 0.5 x 10 x 3 = 15 m². Adding the two parts together, the whole compound-shaped bed covered 40 + 15 = 55 m² -- enough space for a proper family vegetable patch.\n\nA few weeks later, Zin Min helped his class prepare a triangular flag for Sports Day. The flag's base measured 60 cm and its height measured 40 cm, so the fabric needed came to 0.5 x 60 x 40 = 1,200 cm² -- Zin Min converted this to a more useful figure for the tailor, though the tailor was happy to work directly with square centimetres.\n\nFinally, back in the garden, Zin Min's father decided to lay a strip of parallelogram-shaped paving stones along one edge of the new bed. Each paving stone had a base of 20 cm and a perpendicular height of 15 cm, giving an area of 20 x 15 = 300 cm² per stone -- quite different from the triangular flag calculation, since a parallelogram never needs the extra 'half' that a triangle always requires.\n\n\"A triangle is basically half of a parallelogram sharing the same base and height,\" Zin Min explained to his father, comparing the vegetable bed, the flag, and the paving stones all in one afternoon of measuring.",
