        passageEn:
            "Zin Min's family was building a small hexagonal gazebo in the garden, and the carpenter asked Zin Min to double-check some of the angles before the wooden frame was nailed together.\n\nFirst, the carpenter showed Zin Min the triangular roof truss that would sit above the gazebo. Two of its angles measured 65° and 70°. Since the angles in any triangle always add up to 180°, Zin Min calculated the third angle as 180 - 65 - 70 = 45°, exactly matching the angle the carpenter had already cut into the wood.\n\nNext, Zin Min checked the small rectangular door frame for the gazebo's entrance. Three of its angles measured 90°, 90°, and 88°, slightly less than a perfect right angle because of a small measuring error. Since the angles in any quadrilateral always add up to 360°, Zin Min worked out the fourth angle should be 360 - 90 - 90 - 88 = 92°, showing the carpenter exactly where the frame needed a tiny adjustment to close up properly.\n\nFinally, the main structure of the gazebo itself was a regular hexagon, with six equal sides and six equal interior angles. Zin Min used the polygon angle-sum formula, (n - 2) x 180°, with n = 6 sides: (6 - 2) x 180 = 720° for the total of all six interior angles. Since a regular hexagon shares that total equally, he divided 720° by 6 to find each interior angle measured exactly 120°.\n\n\"Whether it's a roof truss, a door frame, or the whole hexagon,\" Zin Min told the carpenter, \"every shape's angles follow their own strict rule, and once you know the rule, you can always find the missing piece.\"",
