            "On a quiet Tuesday morning, Zin Min's whole Year 6 class sat down for a full 90-minute mock mathematics exam, covering every topic from the entire year: number, the four operations, fractions and percentages, ratio, algebra, measurement, geometry, and statistics all mixed together, exactly as the real secondary-entrance exam would be.\n\nZin Min noticed that many questions used specific command words. When a question said 'Calculate', he knew a numerical answer was expected, with working out shown for partial credit. When a question said 'Explain why', he knew a sentence of reasoning was needed, not just a number.\n\nOne tricky question asked him to calculate 40% of 350 kyat. Zin Min worked it out as 40/100 x 350 = 140 kyat, carefully writing each step so that even if his final answer had been wrong, the marker could still see correct method being applied.\n\nAfter the exam, Zin Min's teacher handed back the papers along with the official mark scheme, showing exactly how each mark had been awarded -- one mark for setting up the correct method, another mark for the correct working, and a final mark for the correct final answer. Zin Min realised he had lost one mark on a ratio question not because his final answer was wrong, but because he had forgotten to show the step where he divided the total by the number of parts.\n\n\"A mock exam isn't just about getting the right answer,\" Zin Min told his study group afterwards, \"it's about learning exactly how marks are earned, so that on the real exam, every correct step I write down counts.\"",
