        passageEn:
            "Su Su noticed that railway tracks were a perfect real-world example of parallel lines, and a road crossing the tracks at an angle acted exactly like a transversal in her geometry homework. At the crossing, she measured one angle between the road and a rail as 55°.\n\n\"Can I work out every other angle at this crossing without measuring them all?\" she wondered. Using the corresponding angles rule, she knew the matching angle at the second rail's crossing point was also 55°. Using the fact that angles on a straight line add to 180°, she found the angle right next to her original 55° angle (on the same rail) was 180° - 55° = 125°.\n\nThen she applied the alternate angles rule: the angle diagonally across the tracks from her original 55° angle, forming a 'Z' shape, was also 55°. Finally, she checked the co-interior angle -- on the same side of the road, between the two rails -- which should be supplementary to 55°, giving 180° - 55° = 125°. \"Eight angles total at this crossing,\" Su Su realised, \"but really only two distinct values, 55° and 125°, repeating in a predictable pattern because the rails are parallel.\" Her teacher confirmed this was exactly right, and that this pattern holds at every transversal crossing two parallel lines, no matter the specific angle.",
