        passageEn:
            "For an art-and-maths project, Thiri wanted to recreate a honeycomb pattern using regular hexagon tiles, but first she needed to know each interior angle so the tiles would fit together without gaps. Using the polygon formula (n - 2) × 180°, she calculated the interior angle sum for a hexagon: (6 - 2) × 180° = 4 × 180° = 720°.\n\nSince a regular hexagon has 6 equal interior angles, she divided the total by 6: 720° ÷ 6 = 120° per angle. \"That's why hexagons tile perfectly,\" her teacher explained. \"Three 120° angles meeting at a single point add up to exactly 360°, a full turn, leaving no gaps and no overlaps.\"\n\nThiri wondered whether regular pentagons would work the same way. She calculated a regular pentagon's interior angle: (5 - 2) × 180° ÷ 5 = 540° ÷ 5 = 108°. Trying to fit angles around a point, three 108° angles would only total 324°, leaving a 36° gap -- not a full 360°. \"So pentagons can't tile a flat surface without gaps,\" Thiri concluded, \"but hexagons can, because 120° divides evenly into 360° exactly three times. That's the real reason honeycombs are hexagonal and not pentagonal -- it's the most efficient shape that tiles perfectly.\"",
