        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw build a classroom shape museum to show off everything they learned this term. Moe Moe's exhibit displays 2D shapes: a triangle, a rectangle, a pentagon, and a hexagon, each labeled with its number of sides and corners. \"The hexagon has the most, with 6 sides and 6 corners,\" she explains to visitors.\n\nNay Chi's exhibit is all about 3D shapes. She lines up a cube, a cuboid, a sphere, a cylinder, and a cone, describing their faces, edges, and vertices. \"The cube has 6 flat faces, but the sphere has just 1 curved surface and no flat faces at all,\" she says, letting visitors roll the sphere across the table.\n\nZaw Zaw's exhibit focuses on symmetry and patterns. He shows a folded square with 4 lines of symmetry, a folded equilateral triangle with 3 lines of symmetry, and a repeating pattern of shapes along the exhibit table: square, circle, square, circle. \"Symmetry means both halves match when you fold along the line,\" he tells a group of curious classmates.\n\nTheir teacher walks through the whole museum, delighted. \"You've combined 2D shapes, 3D shapes, and symmetry into one incredible exhibition,\" she says. \"This shows everything from Term 4, working together beautifully.\" The three friends welcome the rest of the school to visit their shape museum.",
        passageMy:
