            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw open a pretend bakery to practice sharing food fairly. Moe Moe cuts a round cake into 2 same-sized pieces to share with a friend. \"Each piece is a half,\" she explains, \"because there are 2 equal parts, and I have 1 of them.\"\n\nNay Chi wants to share a pie among 4 friends, so she cuts it into 4 same-sized slices. \"Each slice is a quarter,\" she says, \"1 out of 4 equal parts.\" She checks carefully that all 4 slices look exactly the same size before serving them.\n\nZaw Zaw has a tricky job: sharing 1 orange among 3 friends. He carefully cuts it into 3 pieces that all look the same size. \"Each piece is a third,\" he announces, \"1 out of 3 equal parts.\" He makes sure no piece is bigger than the others, because that would not be fair sharing.\n\nTheir teacher visits the bakery and checks everyone's cutting. \"You've all cut your food into truly equal parts, which is what makes a fraction fair,\" she says. \"If the pieces aren't the same size, it isn't a real half, quarter, or third.\" The three friends serve their fairly-shared treats to a very happy line of customers.",
