        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw pretend to be math detectives, searching for clue words in word problems to figure out which operation to use. \"The first clue is 'altogether',\" says Moe Moe, reading a problem about 20 red balloons and 15 blue balloons. \"That word tells me to add: 20 plus 15 equals 35 balloons altogether.\"\n\nNay Chi finds a problem with the words 'how many are left'. \"That's a subtraction clue,\" she says. \"If there were 40 candies and 25 got eaten, I subtract: 40 minus 25 equals 15 candies left.\"\n\nZaw Zaw studies a trickier problem: \"6 bags with 4 apples in each bag -- how many apples in total?\" He notices the clue phrase '6 bags with 4 in each'. \"That's a multiplication clue,\" he says, \"because it describes equal groups. 6 times 4 equals 24 apples.\"\n\nTheir teacher watches the three detectives at work. \"You've all learned to spot the clue words that tell you which operation to use,\" she says. \"'Altogether' means add, 'how many are left' means subtract, and 'groups of' means multiply or divide. Being a math detective means reading carefully before you calculate.\" The three friends continue solving mystery problems, proud of their new detective skills.",
