        passageEn:
            "Zin Ko visited a fairground with a large Ferris wheel and became curious about the circle theorems he had just learned at school. He noticed a support cable running from the ground, touching the outer rim of the wheel at a single point, like a tangent line touching a circle. He knew that at that point of contact, the cable must be perpendicular to the wheel's radius, forming a 90° angle. The engineer operating the ride told Zin Ko that the wheel had a radius of 15 m, and the cable stretched from a fixed ground anchor to the point of tangency. If the distance from the ground anchor to the centre of the wheel was 25 m, Zin Ko wanted to work out the length of the cable itself. Since the cable (tangent) was perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, the triangle formed by the ground anchor, the centre of the wheel, and the point of tangency was right-angled. Using Pythagoras: cable² = 25² - 15² = 625 - 225 = 400, so the cable length = 20 m. Later, while riding the wheel, Zin Ko sat at the very top, directly opposite a boarding platform that sat on a diameter line through the centre. He realised that any passenger boarding at ground level and any passenger at the exact opposite point of the diameter, together with a third passenger anywhere else on the wheel's rim, would form a triangle with a 90° angle at the third passenger's position -- exactly the angle-in-a-semicircle theorem in action, just turning slowly through the air.",
        passageMy:
