        passageEn:
            "Hnin loved helping her father in their garden every weekend, but this Saturday's project was different -- they were building a decorative arch over the garden gate, and her father asked her to work out its shape using mathematics. He told her the height of the arch, in meters, followed the equation y = -x^2 + 6x, where x was the horizontal distance from one side of the arch.\n\nHnin decided to make a table of values before drawing anything. She calculated y for x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. At x = 0, y was 0. At x = 1, y was 5. At x = 2, y was 8. At x = 3, y was 9. Then the numbers started falling again: at x = 4, y was 8, at x = 5, y was 5, and at x = 6, y was back to 0.\n\nPlotting these points on graph paper, Hnin watched the curve rise smoothly to a peak and then fall in a perfect mirror image. The highest point, the turning point, was at (3, 9) -- exactly halfway between where the arch met the ground on each side. That meant the line of symmetry was x = 3.\n\nHer father was thrilled. Since the arch reached a maximum height of 9 meters right in the middle, he now knew exactly how much timber and fabric to buy. Hnin smiled, realizing that a single quadratic equation had helped design a real structure in their own garden.",
