            "Thazin Nwe was helping design a scale model of her school's new library for a Founders' Day exhibit. The original blueprint placed the library's four corners at A(2, 2), B(6, 2), C(6, 5), and D(2, 5). To create a small architectural model, she first enlarged the blueprint by scale factor 1/2 from the origin, mapping A to A'(1, 1), B to B'(3, 1), C to C'(3, 2.5), and D to D'(1, 2.5) -- every coordinate was simply halved. She then needed to reposition this smaller model onto a different part of the display table, so she translated it by vector (5, 3), moving A' to A''(6, 4), B' to B''(8, 4), C' to C''(8, 5.5), and D' to D''(6, 5.5) -- every coordinate increased by the same vector. Thazin Nwe checked her work using vector AB from the very first week of the term: in the original blueprint, vector AB = B - A = (6-2, 2-2) = (4, 0). After enlarging by 1/2, the corresponding vector A'B' = (3-1, 1-1) = (2, 0), exactly half of the original -- proof that enlargement affects vector length by the scale factor. After the translation, vector A''B'' was still (2, 0), since a translation moves both endpoints by the same amount and doesn't change the vector between them at all.",
