        passageEn:
            "Sandi Aye was preparing for her school's maths olympiad qualifying round, where every question demanded a full algebraic proof rather than just a numerical answer. The first question asked her to prove that the sum of any two consecutive integers is always odd. Letting the first integer be n, the next consecutive integer was n + 1, so their sum was n + (n + 1) = 2n + 1. Since 2n is always even (any integer multiplied by 2), adding 1 to an even number always gives an odd number -- so 2n + 1 is always odd, no matter what value n takes. This proved the claim for every possible integer at once, rather than checking examples one by one. The second question asked her to prove that (x + 3)² - (x - 3)² is always a multiple of 12 for any whole number x. Expanding both brackets: (x² + 6x + 9) - (x² - 6x + 9) = 12x. Since 12x is always 12 multiplied by a whole number, Sandi Aye had proven it was always a multiple of 12, completing both proofs using only algebra -- never plugging in a single specific number.",
