        passageEn:
            "Aung Htet Paing was helping his village planning committee decide where to build a new well that needed to be exactly the same distance from two nearby villages, marked on a map at A(2, 4) and B(10, 8). He knew any point equidistant from A and B must lie on the perpendicular bisector of AB. First, he found the midpoint of AB: ((2+10)/2, (4+8)/2) = (6, 6). Next, he found the gradient of AB: (8-4)/(10-2) = 4/8 = 1/2. Since the perpendicular bisector's gradient is the negative reciprocal, it had gradient -2. Using the midpoint (6, 6) and gradient -2, Aung Htet Paing wrote the perpendicular bisector's equation: y - 6 = -2(x - 6), which simplifies to y = -2x + 18. Any well built along this line, he explained to the committee, would automatically be the same distance from both villages -- for example, checking the point (6, 6) itself: distance to A is √((6-2)² + (6-4)²) = √(16+4) = √20, and distance to B is √((6-10)² + (6-8)²) = √(16+4) = √20 -- exactly equal, confirming his method worked.",
