        passageEn:
            "Su Yadanar Htun was working through a classic vector geometry proof for her coursework: showing that the line joining the midpoints of two sides of a triangle is always parallel to the third side, and exactly half its length. In triangle OAB, with O as the origin, the position vectors of A and B were a and b. Point M was the midpoint of OA, so its position vector was (1/2)a. Point N was the midpoint of OB, so its position vector was (1/2)b. To find vector MN, Su Yadanar Htun calculated MN = (position vector of N) - (position vector of M) = (1/2)b - (1/2)a = (1/2)(b - a). She then compared this to vector AB, which was AB = (position vector of B) - (position vector of A) = b - a. Since MN = (1/2)(b - a) and AB = (b - a), MN was exactly half of AB as a vector -- meaning MN was parallel to AB (since one is a scalar multiple of the other) and exactly half its length. This proved the midpoint theorem using nothing but vector algebra, without measuring a single length or angle directly.",
        passageMy:
