            "For the Nova School fun fair, Kaung Sett ran a measurement-themed booth all day, and he tracked every unit he used. In the morning, he started with a 5 m roll of ribbon for prize decorations and cut off 240 cm, leaving 260 cm on the roll -- a length task. Around midday, he weighed the prize sweets: a 1 kg 300 g bag combined with a 900 g bag came to 2200 g in total -- a mass task. In the afternoon, he refilled the juice station: a 3 l jug poured into 500 ml cups gave exactly 6 full cups for visitors -- a capacity task. By closing time, Kaung Sett had used length for ribbon and distances, mass for rice and sweets, and capacity for juice and water, and he noticed the exact same trick worked for all three: every metric unit was built from a smaller unit and a larger unit connected by a fixed multiplier -- 100 for length, and 1000 for both mass and capacity -- so multiplying or dividing by that number let him convert and compare no matter which part of the booth he was measuring.",
